  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe dry pattern that has been in place for the last week plus\nwill mostly continue into the weekend as high pressure and\nridging dominates the region. \n\nBehind an earlier passing cold front, a drier and briefly cooler\nairmass will advect into the region. This will cause reductions\nto Td/RH values today and combined with the dry pattern for the\nlast week plus, some fire weather concerns will continue.\nHowever, winds are expected to remain mostly on the lighter \nside, helping to limit any more elevated concerns. \n\nDry weather will continue through the day on Thursday ahead of\nan approaching cold front. The cold front, extending southwest\nfrom a sfc low tracking from the Upper Great Lakes into Canada,\nwill cross the area Thursday night into Friday morning. Guidance\nis starting to trend slightly wetter with the passing front, but\nmost of the showers should be near/north of I90 for Thursday\nnight into Friday morning. Ahead of the approaching cold front,\ntemperatures and dewpoint values will increase each day for \nWednesday and Thursday as southerly flow develops. \n\nMainly dry conditions are expected for Friday afternoon through\nat least most of Sunday afternoon. A sfc low will track across \nthe Ohio Valley and across the Central Great Lakes, spreading an\narea of showers across the region. This appears to mark a \ntransitions to a wetter pattern starting early next week. There \nis still uncertainty in track of sfc low and timing, but it does\nappear as though our stretch of dry weather will come to an end\nlate Sunday into early next week.",
